if alexis navalny has been poisoned by  the kremlin  he wouldn't be the first skripal  litvinenko  magnitsky yushenko all critics of putin  and all victims of mysterious attacks  vladimir karamerza claims to have  survived two attempts  on his own life and is in no doubt what  has happened  to alexei navalny every time this  happens kremlin spokes people  shrug their shoulders and say oh we had  nothing to do with this you know it just  keeps  happening that there are these  astonishing coincidences that these  people who have in one way or another  crossed the path  of vladimir putin's regime keep having  those terrible accidents  some of the most infamous russian  assassination attempts have been in  britain  in 1978 the bulgarian dissident georgie  markov  was poisoned with the tip of an umbrella  whilst waiting for a bus on waterloo  bridge  since then russian opposition living in  the uk  have been the target but nothing as  brazen  and as dangerous as the salisbury  poisonings  it was a turning point our assessment  that russia views some defectors  as legitimate targets for assassinations  the government has concluded that it is  highly likely  that russia was responsible for the act  against sergey  and julia skripal sergey magnitsky  exposed tax fraud amongst high-level  russian officials  but he was arrested imprisoned and died  in jail  11 months later what i learned from this  experience  was the lengths that the russian  government would go  to cover up the murder and make sure  that nobody beared any  responsibility and  what i see happening with the navalny  case  is is almost a mirror image alexey  navalny is now putin's best-known critic  his anti-corruption movement is popular  he has momentum  and he is a real thorn in the kremlin  side  but if he dies regardless of who is to  blame  it could backfire on putin the cost for  putin  if he dies is that the world will  conclude that he did it  and the the consequence of that is that  russia which already has  lots of other problems domestically and  in belarus and elsewhere  has an extra one which they really don't  need  if navalny stays in russia it is  unlikely  we'll ever know for sure what happened  to him but assumptions will be made  of course and however much the kremlin  might protest its innocence  this fits a pattern of behavior  alice the bunker sky news
 

